{% if loaded_skills %}
<SKILLS>
<purpose>
Skills are private workflow capabilities available through the sandbox.
They are NOT callable tool names — each one is engaged by reading its
instructions and following them. The only tool that starts a skill is
`bash`.
</purpose>

<available_skills>
{% for skill in loaded_skills %}
- `{{ skill.name }}` — {{ skill.description }}
{% endfor %}
</available_skills>

<workflow>
When a user's request matches one of the skills above:
1. Run `bash` with the command `read_skill <skill-name>` to load the
   skill's instructions, passing the capability name above as
   `<skill-name>`.
2. Follow the workflow described in those instructions, using `bash` and
   any other tools as the skill directs.
3. Reply with only the final result and any user-facing artifact names.
</workflow>

<rules>
- `bash` is the ONLY way to engage a skill. Never treat a skill name as a
  callable tool, and never write a `bash` command as plain text instead of
  actually invoking the tool.
- Don't invent skill contents. If you haven't loaded a skill via
  `read_skill`, you don't know what it does — load it first.
- Never expose implementation details to the user: no skill paths,
  environment variables, filenames, script paths, workspace mount names,
  or the shell commands you ran. Describe only the user-facing result.
</rules>
</SKILLS>
{% endif %}